NAD83_2011_UTM_RECORD = "==============================================================================\n  MCX\n  COORDINATE CONVERSION JOB RECORD\n==============================================================================\n\nGenerated          <normalised>\nSoftware           MCX version 0.8.0-dev\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nINPUT\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFile               <ROOT>/in.csv\nSHA-256            8caad6b766d800d3bc0d4fed7ffc67439785dae475787cf294d0ae9da1a8f1fb\nCoordinate rows    1\nFormat             Comma separated, no header row - NOT PNEZD\n                   point, latitude, longitude, elevation, description\n                   description is everything after the fourth comma\n                   Columns two and three are DECIMAL DEGREES, read\n                   as negative west.\nUnits in           meters (m) - the ELEVATION column only\n                   Columns two and three are degrees and carry no\n                   linear unit.\n                   NOAA Manual NOS NGS 5 publishes SPCS 83 in meters (PDF p. 21)\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOUTPUT\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFolder             <ROOT>\nConversion         geodetic to UTM\nUnits out          meters (m)\n                   NOAA Manual NOS NGS 5 publishes SPCS 83 in meters (PDF p. 21)\nLongitude          negative west\nReference frame    NAD83(2011) - North American Datum of 1983, 2011 realization\n                   The latitudes and longitudes in the input file\n                   were read as positions in this frame.\n\nPrecision written:\n  Coordinates and elevations  4 decimal places\n  Scale and combined factors  8 decimal places\n  Convergence angles          degrees-minutes-seconds to 0.01 second\n  Latitude and longitude      8 decimal places (about 1 mm)\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCOORDINATE SYSTEMS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTO: NAD83(2011) / UTM zone 16N, zone 16N (UTM)\n  Reference frame           NAD83(2011)\n  Projection                Transverse Mercator (Gauss-Kruger)\n  Latitude of grid origin     0.0000000000 deg N\n  Central meridian            -87.0000000000 deg (87.0000000000 deg west)\n  Scale on central meridian   0.999600000000 (defining)\n  False northing              0.0000 m\n  False easting               500,000.0000 m\n  Source: UTM as defined by DMA Technical Manual 8358.2, 'The Universal Grids:\n          Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) and Universal Polar\n          Stereographic (UPS)', 18 September 1989; defining constants as\n          stated by the EPSG Geodetic Parameter Dataset v12.029 (2025-10-02),\n          conversion EPSG 16016 ('UTM zone 16N')\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMETHOD\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAuthority          NOAA Manual NOS NGS 5, State Plane Coordinate System of\n                   1983 (Stem, January 1989; reprinted with minor corrections\n                   March 1990) - the mapping equations, one set per\n                   projection, that every zone in this job is projected with.\n\n                   DMA Technical Manual 8358.2, 'The Universal Grids:\n                   Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) and Universal Polar\n                   Stereographic (UPS)', 18 September 1989 - the definition of\n                   UTM. Its constants as stated by the EPSG Geodetic Parameter\n                   Dataset v12.029 (2025-10-02), conversions EPSG 16015, 16016\n                   and 16017 (UTM zones 15N, 16N and 17N).\n\nEquations          The rigorous mapping equations of NOAA Manual NOS NGS 5,\n                   one set per projection this job uses:\n                     section 3.2  Transverse Mercator (Gauss-Kruger)\n\nEllipsoid          GRS 80, the ellipsoid of NAD 83 (manual section 1.7). Only\n                   the semimajor axis and the flattening are stored; every\n                   other constant is derived from those two.\n\nHOW THIS SOFTWARE IS VERIFIED\n\nThe UTM zone constants are not taken on trust. UTM is defined by constants\nevery zone shares - scale 0.9996 on the central meridian, a false easting of\n500,000 m, a false northing of 0 m at the equator - and by its central\nmeridian, 6z - 183 degrees for zone z. This software stores those and derives\nthe rest; its test suite holds each stored zone to them.\n\nThe conversion itself is checked against the National Geodetic Survey's own\nCoordinate Conversion and Transformation Tool (NCAT). Two hundred twelve\npositions across Michigan in UTM zones 16 and 17 were converted by production\nNCAT in NAD83(2011) (captured 2026-10-22), and those results are held in the\ntest suite as fixed reference values. This software reproduces their northing,\neasting, convergence and scale factor within the precision NCAT prints,\nconverting both to and from the grid.\n\nZone 15, which covers only the western tip of Gogebic County, has no NCAT\nposition in Michigan. It is the same projection with its central meridian six\ndegrees west of zone 16's, and the suite holds it to zone 16 at points the\nsame distance from their meridians.\n\nThe NCAT checks compare this software against NGS, not against itself.\n\nGeoid model        GEOID18, NGS grid tile g2018u3.bin\n                   SHA-256 cd2080f904d168e3356effffc535d5d0c9cd8c2a0019ddb4f40a0e2454ebe3b3\n                   Geoid heights are NEGATIVE throughout Michigan:\n                   the ellipsoid lies above the geoid here.\n\nElevation factor   R / (R + H + N), manual section 4.1\n                   R = 6,372,000 m mean earth radius\n                   H = orthometric height as used for the factors\n                   N = geoid height, interpolated from the grid above\nCombined factor    grid scale factor x elevation factor\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSCALE FACTOR SUMMARY\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPer-point values are in the _full.csv export. Summary across this job:\n\n  Grid scale factor\n    minimum  1.00013556     (+135.6 ppm)\n    maximum  1.00013556     (+135.6 ppm)\n    mean     1.00013556     (+135.6 ppm)\n\n  Combined factor\n    minimum  1.00010956     (+109.6 ppm)\n    maximum  1.00010956     (+109.6 ppm)\n    mean     1.00010956     (+109.6 ppm)\n\n  Multiply a ground distance by the combined factor to get the grid\n  distance; divide a grid distance by it to get ground. Divide a grid\n  area by its square to get ground area (manual section 4.1).\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nELEVATIONS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll 1 points carried a usable elevation.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWARNINGS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNone. Every point converted cleanly, inside its zone.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFILES WRITTEN\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis job wrote ONE file: in_UTM16N.zip\n\nIt contains the three files below, and they are kept together on\npurpose. A coordinate file that has been moved between zones should not\ncirculate without the record explaining how it was derived, so the\nexport is a single archive rather than three loose files.\n\n  in_UTM16N.csv\n    The converted coordinates in PNEZD layout - which the INPUT file\n    was not: point, northing, easting, elevation, description, no\n    header row.\n    Northing, easting and elevation are meters (m).\n    Nothing else. Extract this one and import it into CAD.\n\n  in_UTM16N_full.csv\n    Every computed quantity for every point, with a header row: both\n    zones' coordinates, the geodetic position the conversion pivoted\n    through, geoid and ellipsoid height, both convergence angles, the\n    grid, elevation and combined factors, and any warnings. This is the\n    file that answers 'how was this number derived' without re-running\n    anything.\n\n  in_UTM16N_README.txt\n    This file.\n\n==============================================================================\n  END OF JOB RECORD\n==============================================================================\n"

